Practical No. 11
Handling Request Data – @PathVariable, @RequestParam and @RequestBody
Textbook Reference: Chapter 6 — Building RESTful Microservices
Objective
Correctly use the three main ways a Spring REST controller receives client input: @PathVariable for
identifying a resource, @RequestParam for optional query filters, and @RequestBody for JSON payloads.
Built with Spring Boot, Spring Data JPA, Maven, and an embedded H2 database.
Project Setup Summary
Build tool: Maven | Framework: Spring Boot | Database: H2 (in-memory) | Base package:
com.example.customerapi
Package layout: entity, dto, repository, controller.
Note: the class list specifies create(@RequestBody CustomerDTO), so a small CustomerDTO (name, city,
age — no id) is used for the POST payload and mapped to the Customer entity inside the controller before
saving.
